Z||000000||chuckjr||07-15-2001||01:35 AM||||Hi , let me start by saying this is a wonderful site! I have solved alot of my problems here.<BR>Let me start by saying i have a 1985 fiero with a 2.8 v6 four speed. I got this car for $100.00 from friend (lucky me). He blew the first motor so he installed a buick v6 (from what he said). They did a hack job on the wiring and vacume lines . But not i have them all strait. The problem im having is that the car WILL NOT IDLE . I have replaced all the sensors TPS , IAC , O2 , MAP , EVEN REPAIRED EGR TUBE . Im sure i forgot a few more. I checked for all vacume leaks and to no avail. I even tested TPS with volt meter.The car is hard starting and when i do get it started it will run for a sec. and stall. I was adjusting the timing and found that while the a+b termanals were shorted the car idled fine . GO FIGURE ! Can someone please help. I am at witts end with this car...but i do like it and want to fix it right! thank you chuck||||reg||5
Z||000001||starfighter007@msn.com||07-15-2001||01:18 PM||||not trying to be a wise guy or anything.why not short the a b terminals and leave it like that? I mean if what you are saying that when you short these terminals the car runs good I would maybe leave it like that unless someone here on the forum has a different solution just my .02cents.||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||07-15-2001||02:21 PM||||First of all, Kansans are <B>not</B> allowed on this Forum.  Go away  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>To me, a "Buick V6" means a "gallon V6," that is, the 231 cubic inch or 3.8 liter or 3800, 90-degree V6.  I'd bet your next paycheck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> that isn't what you've got.  Just a hunch, because it wouldn't be an easy homespun swap.  It sure would be easier if we knew what engine you've got.  <P>If it starts and idles better in the diagnostic mode, you might want to find out why.  The easiest thing to start with, is to disable the EGR.  Disconnect and plug the vacuum line to the EGR valve, and see if anything improves.<P>Next, what does the timing light tell you whrn you check it in diag. mode ("A" connected to "B")?  Does the timing change when you take it out of disag. mode (that is, disconnect A & B)?  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Mach10||07-15-2001||03:19 PM||||Get the VIN/S/N from the engine block. It'd really help to know what motor you're using. You might have to rip the system apart to sort out gremlins. if it runs in diagnostic mode (even badly) then a sensor or two might not be wired in correctly.<BR>Oh, and welcome to the forum. )||||reg||7
Z||000004||chuckjr||07-15-2001||04:58 PM||||Ok......heres the scoop the kid really dosnt know what the motor came from...it looks like it is a 2.8 . I went back out this morning shot 1/2 can of carb cleaner in the throdle body and its better....now the problem is once it gets started and i set the timing it runs perfect on idle with a+b shorted and with out! But when i shut the motor off and try t start it again it WILL NOT START unless i move the timing , then it will start and run rough , then set the timing correct and idles smooth..Could i still have the distributor a tooth off or so? Again i thank all of you for this help. ||||reg||1
